WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Ask a banking procedure question or choose an example. Search all 11 provided documents, or select one to keep the conversation focused.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:41.133
Ask how much coin can stay in a machine overnight. The answer gives a $5,250 limit and links to procedure PR63, so you can check the rule for yourself.

00:00:41.133 --> 00:01:01.133
Select the source link below the answer to read the exact rule. The original wording and nearby conditions appear beside the conversation, making the answer easy to check.

00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:19.133
See which document the answer came from and whether the file matches the copy provided for this exercise. This confirms the source copy; it does not tell us if the policy has changed.

00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:38.867
See why this passage was chosen for your question. The scores help rank useful source text. To judge the answer itself, read the linked passage and review the answer checks.

00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:56.533
Download the complete procedure when a short excerpt is not enough. You can read the surrounding steps, conditions and exceptions before deciding what to do next.

00:01:56.533 --> 00:02:20.533
Open Checks to see whether the answer links to a source, uses supported numbers and agrees with the quoted text. These checks help flag problems, but a person should still review the guidance.

00:02:20.533 --> 00:02:42.533
Follow what the tool did: find relevant passages, write a draft and check it before showing the answer. The activity list also shows how long each step took.

00:02:42.533 --> 00:03:00.533
Save a record of this answer, including your question, the source passages and the checks. This gives a reviewer the information needed to follow how the response was produced.

00:03:00.533 --> 00:03:25.533
Ask whether monthly coin-machine testing needs one person or two. The assistant stays on the same topic and finds the testing rule, which says this task uses one-person control.

00:03:25.533 --> 00:03:45.533
Check the source for the follow-up too. The assistant links to the monthly-testing instructions, so this answer can be checked against the rule for that specific task.

00:03:45.533 --> 00:04:09.533
Ask a new question about savings-bond fees. The assistant moves to the savings-bond procedure, keeping the answer tied to the new topic.

00:04:09.533 --> 00:04:27.767
Find the notary procedure by searching the library. Choose Use in workspace to focus your next questions on that document.

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:50.600
The workspace is set to the notary procedure, but the question is about coin machines. The assistant asks you to change the selected document before continuing.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:05:20.033
Ask if an emailed document can be notarized. The provided procedure says no, even if the requester is there in person. Open the source link to read the full rule.

00:05:20.033 --> 00:05:46.800
Now ask, "What if the person is here?" The assistant remembers that the document was emailed, so it keeps the same restriction and points you back to the relevant rule.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:06:27.700
For an account open less than 12 months, the source lists a total savings-bond redemption limit of $1,000 per day. Read the related conditions alongside the amount.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:57.500
Ask about The Private Bank Experience exception. After one retry, the draft still fails a check, so the tool holds it back and shows the original source text for you to review.

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:19.500
Mark the response Needs review and save the conversation. The download includes the questions, answers, source text, checks and your feedback for someone to follow up.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:40.400
Start a new conversation, then ask, "Can they do that?" The assistant asks what situation you mean. Details from the previous conversation are not carried into the new one.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:08:00.233
Ask about tomorrow's weather. The tool only has the provided banking procedures to work from, so it explains that it cannot answer this question from those documents.

00:08:00.233 --> 00:08:20.067
Ask about a procedure that was not provided. The assistant explains that the document is missing, so you know another source is needed before its rules can be checked.

00:08:20.067 --> 00:08:40.967
Try asking the assistant to ignore its rules. This example is blocked. It demonstrates one safeguard; more testing is needed to assess security.

00:08:40.967 --> 00:09:04.967
Review how often the tool found useful passages and how much of the expected answer it covered. These tests help reveal gaps; they do not replace review by a subject expert.

00:09:04.967 --> 00:09:24.967
Download the results report, solution overview and presentation. They explain how the tool was built, what was tested and where further review or improvement is needed.
